ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: Dataset publishers who call `datasets.Image().encode_example` on in-memory PIL objects during `push_to_hub`, and downstream benchmark consumers who may (now or later) honor EXIF orientation (HF dataset viewer, browsers, `PIL.ImageOps.exif_transpose`, `torchvision.io.decode_jpeg(apply_exif_orientation=True)`). Also reproducibility auditors tracking silent cross-mirror score drift.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Microbenchmark-scale at measured real-world prevalence; materiality only in high-EXIF regimes. At Open Images V7 val f=2.75% the cross-mirror fork is 1.16pp (packet: "Composed cross-mirror SCORE FORK = f x flip: 1.16pp @ f=2.75% (real)"). The 2pp+ band requires f≥5% (2.1pp @5%, 4.2pp @10%, 10.5pp @25%), which matches phone-roll literature (3-25%) but not curated benchmarks—8 HF parquet benchmarks scanned have f~0 "BECAUSE their EXIF was already destroyed at publish" (packet). Under default loaders (PIL Image.open, torchvision decode_jpeg apply_exif_orientation=False) both mirrors score identically; the fork is "INVISIBLE until any consumer flips the EXIF bit" (packet). Per-tagged-image flip is 42.1pp (packet: "M1 (passthrough, tag=6) consumer-EXIF toggle = 42.1pp"), but prevalence dilutes the operational win.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [HF datasets Image.encode_example re-serializes via PIL.Image.save which strips EXIF by default—changing this is a breaking behavior change for existing pipelines; file-PATH encode preserves EXIF but PIL-object path is common in programmatic publishing; default production eval loaders ignore EXIF (torchvision apply_exif_orientation=False stable 0.15->0.27, PIL default ignores) so the fix is invisible to most current scores; no dataset-card diff signals the destruction ("no pixel change, no dataset-card diff" per claim); publisher education required to use path-based push or manually preserve exif=; EXIF-honoring consumer population is niche—most eval harnesses do not transpose].
RATIONALE: The mechanism is verified and distinct from the known display footgun: "datasets.Image().encode_example(in-memory PIL) DESTROYS the tag: input orient=6 -> stored bytes orient=None (re-serializes via PIL.Image.save, no exif=)" and "encode_example(file PATH) PRESERVES it (orient=6)" (packet STEP-0). Controls held: "identity-orientation (tag=1) = 0.0pp all toggles/mirrors" and "JPEG-requantization-only (re-encode of tag-1) = 0.0pp drift" (packet), isolating orientation destruction from codec noise. Irreversibility and invisibility are load-bearing: M2 (datasets re-encode, tag destroyed) toggle = 0.0pp, and the fork is invisible under default loaders. However, operational significance is limited because the realistic f=2.75% yields only 1.16pp fork, below the pre-registered 2pp "it-matters" bar; the claim's materiality rests on the higher-prevalence regime (f≥5%) which is honest for phone-roll data but not for the 8 HF benchmarks already stripped in the wild. Adoption requires either (a) HF datasets to preserve EXIF on the PIL path (risking backwards incompatibility) or (b) publishers to avoid the PIL-object path—neither is a drop-in win for production pipelines that already ignore EXIF. The negative result is actionable guidance: "publish via file path or preserve exif= to avoid silent cross-mirror non-reproducibility; consumers must lock EXIF handling"—a hygiene fix, not a performance advantage.
